# ================================
# Condor: can a lower-dimensional projection rescue the mixture at d=256?
#   condor_submit scripts/xai/submit/select_k_v4_pca_aggressive.sub
#
# d=256 is settled as the encoder: it is the best detector by a wide margin
# (AUC 0.9823 +- 0.0042 on HH->4b against 0.9373 +- 0.0123 at d64, ~3.5 sigma),
# so the interpretation has to live there. What is not settled is whether the
# GMM has to see all 256 dimensions. It does not: the AE — and therefore the
# anomaly score and every AD number in the paper — is trained on the full
# embedding, and only the clustering step is projected. Reducing the dimension
# here changes the interpretation's resolution, never the detector.
#
# The v3 PCA run tested a 0.99 variance target, which was the wrong knob: VCReg's
# variance term keeps the spectrum nearly flat, so 0.99 left 209 of 256 dimensions
# — an 18% cut that kept the conditioning problem intact — and the ARI got worse
# at every K (0.55-0.65 against 0.60-0.72 unprojected).
#
# Two effects are confounded in that result and this grid separates them. The
# projection truncates, which should help a mixture by improving conditioning, and
# it rotates onto principal axes, which need not help at all: a diagonal
# covariance is not rotation-invariant, and the v3 result says the embedding's own
# axes suit it better than principal ones do. At 209 dimensions truncation is
# negligible, so v3 measured the rotation penalty almost alone. Scanning 16/32/64
# lets truncation grow until it either overcomes that penalty or does not.
#
# Read the outcome as: ARI rising towards 0.80 as dimensions fall means the low
# stability was conditioning and a defensible K exists in the projected space,
# with full covariance affordable again as a bonus. ARI flat or falling means the
# ambiguity is in the geometry itself, not in the dimension count, and the K-as-
# resolution argument stands on its own — a clean negative result either way.
#
# Same grid, restarts and n_init as the unprojected d256 run, so all five points
# (16, 32, 64, 209, 256) are directly comparable.
# ================================

executable = scripts/xai/submit/wrapper_select_k_v2.sh
arguments  = $(ClusterId)

log_dir = /afs/cern.ch/user/d/dgenoves/foundation_model_testing_for_AD/logs/condor_logs/xai
output  = $(log_dir)/select_k_v4_d256_pca$(PCADIM)_$(ClusterId).out
error   = $(log_dir)/select_k_v4_d256_pca$(PCADIM)_$(ClusterId).err
log     = $(log_dir)/select_k_v4_d256_pca$(PCADIM)_$(ClusterId).log

run_as_owner   = True
+JobFlavour    = "nextweek"
getenv         = True
# Deliberately below the v3 runs' 16 CPU / 64 GB. That request is rounded up to
# 24 CPU / 66 GB and routed to the `bigmcore` hostgroup, which has 10 slots in the
# whole pool and rarely a free one — the v3 PCA job sat idle for 7 hours before
# matching. Nothing here needs it: the GMM runs on at most 64 dimensions, and the
# only heavy step is the PCA fit itself (1.15M x 256, a few GB peak).
request_cpus   = 8
request_memory = 24576

SEED   = 3
DMODEL = 256
environment = "SEED=$(SEED) DMODEL=$(DMODEL) NRESTARTS=12 COVTYPE=diag PCAVAR=$(PCADIM) OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 MKL_NUM_THREADS=8"

queue PCADIM in (16 32 64)
